{"id":"b35","n":35,"title_ko":"Yet To Come","title_en":"Yet To Come","album":"Proof","year":2022,"yt":"kXpOEzNZ8hQ","hook":[17,18,19,20,21,22,23,24],"notes":{"s":"The best years are being claimed for the future, not the past.","t":"The lead single from Proof, the 2022 anthology that closed out the group's first chapter and preceded their military enlistments, so every line about what has not happened yet was read as a promise to come back. The verses push back on being called the greatest: the titles feel heavy, the praise is embarrassing, and the rapper claims he is still writing the way he did at thirteen. The video was shot at an airport in Gyeonggi province and ends with the nine years of their career treated as a long circle returning to where it started. ARMY read the title as the group's answer to everyone assuming the story was over.","k":[["난 변화는 많았지만 변함은 없었다 해","Two nouns pulled off the same verb 변하다, to change. 변화 is change as an event that happened, 변함 is changing as an ongoing state, and 변함없다 is the set phrase for steadfast. So the line says a great deal happened and nothing moved, built out of grammar rather than contradiction, and no English rendering keeps both halves."],["최고란 말은 아직까지 낯간지러워","낯 is the face and 간지럽다 is to be ticklish, so the compound is the squirm you feel when a compliment lands on you. English cringe is broader; this one is specifically about being praised, and it is the polite way a Korean public figure declines a superlative."],["그날을 향해 더 우리답게","-답게 attaches to a noun and means in the manner proper to that thing: 나답게 like myself, 사람답게 as a human being should, 학생답게 as befits a student. The song's last word is this suffix rather than a boast, and it is one of the highest-value endings for a learner to recognise."],["지금 난 마치 열세 살","Thirteen is given as a fact rather than a metaphor, pointing back at the age the verse's writer started making rap. 마치 normally sets up 같다 or 처럼 later in the sentence, and lyrics routinely drop the second half and let the comparison hang."]]},"vocab":[{"w":"부지런히","en":"diligently, without slacking","note":"Adverb of 부지런하다; the opposite is 게으르다, lazy","i":2},{"w":"아름답다","en":"to be beautiful","note":"Written register; 예쁘다 is what you say about a face or a bag","i":3},{"w":"향하다","en":"to face, to head toward","note":"-을 향해 is the standard way to say toward a goal","i":7},{"w":"벅차다","en":"to be more than one can hold","note":"숨이 벅차다 is breathless from exertion, 가슴이 벅차다 is overwhelmed with feeling","i":7},{"w":"최고","en":"the best, the greatest","note":"Also used as everyday praise, 이거 최고야, this is the best","i":11},{"w":"무겁다","en":"to be heavy","note":"Here about the weight of a title, the same metaphor as English","i":13},{"w":"그저","en":"just, merely, simply","note":"Lowers the claim; 그냥 is its colloquial cousin","i":15},{"w":"여전하다","en":"to be just as before, unchanged","note":"여전히 as an adverb means still, and is one of the most useful words in the list","i":18},{"w":"소년","en":"boy, youth","note":"Literary; a child you are talking to is 아이 or 애","i":18},{"w":"꿈꾸다","en":"to dream","note":"Built from 꿈, a dream, plus 꾸다, which only exists to go with it","i":21},{"w":"숨죽이다","en":"to hold one's breath, to keep still","i":23},{"w":"멈추다","en":"to stop, to come to a halt","i":23},{"w":"시작","en":"start, beginning","note":"시작이야 is how Korean announces that something is now beginning","i":27},{"w":"수식어","en":"modifier, epithet","note":"A grammar term for an attributive word, used here for the labels pinned on them","i":28},{"w":"낯간지럽다","en":"to be embarrassing to be told, cringey","i":29},{"w":"변화","en":"change, transformation","note":"변화 is change that occurred; 변함 from the same verb is the state of changing, and 변함없다 means unwavering","i":34},{"w":"순간","en":"moment, instant","note":"순간적으로 means momentarily, in a flash","i":36},{"w":"채우다","en":"to fill","note":"Causative of 차다, to be full; also used for fastening a belt or a button","i":40},{"w":"제자리","en":"one's original plac